-- Passage rg8-argument-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8746a211-e31f-5ef9-b9c0-c8ab12fc9639', 'rg8-argument-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-argument-evidence'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Trade the Lawn for a Native Garden$q$, $q$The wide front lawn at Pinecrest Middle School looks neat, but it wastes water and offers little to wildlife. The school should replace half of the lawn with a garden of native plants, the kinds that grew in this region before the town was built.

Native plants need far less water than grass. They are adapted to local rainfall, so once their roots are established, they rarely need watering. The county water office estimates that a native garden uses about 60 percent less water than a lawn of the same size. Last summer, Pinecrest watered its lawn three times a week.

Native gardens also help pollinators such as bees and butterflies. When the Elm Park community center planted native flowers, volunteers counted the insects visiting each week. After two years, they recorded more than four times as many bees as they had seen on the old lawn.

The garden could become an outdoor classroom, too. Science classes could study insects, soil and plant growth just steps from the front door.

The current lawn was planted in 1985, the same year the school built its new gym. Everyone who visits a native garden ends up loving it.

Some parents worry that a native garden will look messy. A garden can be designed with clear borders, a stone path and labeled sections. That way, it looks cared for and gives visitors a reason to stop and read.

A native garden would save water, support wildlife and give students a living laboratory. Pinecrest should plant one this spring.$q$, 'draft')
