   $q$Which evidence gives the strongest support for the claim that volunteer counts are accurate?$q$, $q$[{"text":"New counters often walk with an experienced partner before counting alone.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"weak-evidence"},{"text":"Many people enjoy birdwatching as a relaxing hobby on weekends.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"irrelevant-detail"},{"text":"Thousands of volunteers walk through parks, fields and backyards each winter.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"weak-evidence"},{"text":"Volunteer totals matched biologists' totals within 10 percent at 27 of 30 sites.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Volunteer totals matched biologists' totals within 10 percent at 27 of 30 sites.$q$, $q$The comparison study directly tests volunteer counts against expert counts and finds close matches. Training with a partner may help accuracy, but it does not measure whether the counts are actually correct.$q$, 'draft', 'ecc248a0-2807-54eb-b802-b752f8eaa63d')
